<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mammad Amin Rasulzade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1884–1955</span> · Also known as Mammad Amin Rasulzadeh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mammad Amin Rasulzade was an Azerbaijani politician and writer, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musavat">Musavat</a> party and chairman of the national council that proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> Democratic Republic in 1918, the first parliamentary republic in the Muslim world. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> overthrew the republic in 1920 he went into exile and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mammad-amin-rasulzade.jpg" alt="Mammad Amin Rasulzade" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dilgam Ahmad Collection · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahammad_Amin_Rasulzade_5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
